%U A400194 3,1,4,1,12,1,2,2,3,2,4,1,10,1,2,1,12,1,2,1,8,1,12,2,3,1,2,1,12,1,2,2,9
%N A400194 Maximum number of terms in a 2-dense sublist of divisors of n.
%C A400194 a(n) is also the largest number in the n-th row of A384222.
%C A400194 Conjecture: a(n) is the product of the ruler function of n and the maximum number of odd terms in a 2-dense sublist of divisors of n.
%H A400194 Paolo Xausa, <a href="/A400194/b400194.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A400194 a(n) = A001511(n) * A400195(n).
%F A400194 Proof of the formula: Write n = 2^e * m with m odd, so A001511(n) = e + 1. If an even divisor x belongs to a 2-dense sublist S, then x/2 is also a divisor of n. Since x / (x/2) = 2, no gap between consecutive divisors between x/2 and x can exceed 2, so x/2 must belong to S. By induction, each divisor in S shares the same sublist with its odd part d. Conversely, d, 2*d, ..., 2^e*d all belong to S. Thus every 2-dense sublist S is partitioned into complete binary fibers of length e + 1 = A001511(n), each containing exactly one odd divisor. This proves the row identity A384222(n, k) = A001511(n) * A384225(n, k) for all n, k >= 1. Taking the maximum over k yields a(n) = A001511(n) * A400195(n) for all n >= 1. - _Hriday Rungta_, Oct 12 2026
%e A400194 For n = 70 the divisors of 70 are 1, 2, 5, 7, 10, 14, 35, 70. There are three 2-dense sublists of divisors, they are [1, 2], [5, 7, 10, 14], [35, 70]. The first sublist has two terms. The second sublist has four terms. The third sublist has two terms. Therefore the maximum number of terms in a 2-dense sublist is 4, so a(70) = 4.
%t A400194 A400194[n_] := Max[Map[Length, Split[Divisors[n], #2 <= 2*# &]]];
%t A400194 Array[A400194, 100] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Oct 04 2026 *)
%Y A400194 Cf. A001511, A237271, A384222, A384225, A400195.
%K A400194 nonn,new
%O A400194 1,2
